From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 11:49:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] cmd/make: build on a non-illumos host

make(1) is the one program in the gate that cannot be built by the gate's
own makefiles on a machine that does not already have it: every other
makefile here is written in dmake's dialect, and dmake is this program.  On
an illumos build machine that is a non-problem, because dmake is installed.
Anywhere else it is the whole difficulty of getting a first build started.

So add `usr/src/cmd/make/Makefile.bootstrap`, written in the common subset
of make dialects, which builds and installs make(1) against the *host's*
libc using whatever make the host ships.  It is deliberately not reachable
from `usr/src/cmd/make/Makefile`; the normal build keeps using
`Makefile.com`, which knows about CTF, the proto area and message
catalogues, none of which exist yet at bootstrap time.

`lib/vroot/mount.cc` and `lib/vroot/utimes.cc` are left out of that build.
Neither `mount_vroot()` nor `utimes_vroot()` is called from anywhere, and
both files re-declare the libc entry point they wrap using the illumos
signature, which a foreign libc does not share.

The source changes are what a foreign libc needs:

- `<mksh/hostcompat.h>` (new) supplies `timestruc_t`, `__NORETURN`,
  `MAXNAMELEN`, `LOADAVG_1MIN` and `getprogname()`.  It is empty on illumos.

- `<sys/systeminfo.h>` was included for a `sysinfo()` call that is not
  there.

- `__x86` is an illumos-gcc spelling; `__i386__`/`__x86_64__` are every
  gcc's.

- `<sys/loadavg.h>` is illumos-only; getloadavg(3C) itself is declared by
  `<stdlib.h>` everywhere, and `LOADAVG_1MIN` moves to the compat header.

- `/proc/self/path/a.out` is how a process finds its own executable on
  illumos; elsewhere it is `/proc/self/exe`.

- `environ` needs to be declared `extern "C"`, which is also what illumos
  `<unistd.h>` does.

- The leading `-` on the getopt(3C) option strings asks Solaris for nothing
  in particular -- getopt() stops at the first operand, which is what the
  option loop is written against, since it skips operands itself and blanks
  spent options out of argv[].  GNU getopt() gives a leading `-` a meaning,
  "return operands as option 1", whereupon the loop blanks out every target
  named on the command line and make exits with "No arguments to build".
  `+` is the GNU spelling of what illumos does by default.

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5 (claude-opus-5)
